-- title:
--   §4, general TSP local-search paragraph
-- statement:
--   Suppose general TSP has a uniform effective polynomial-size neighborhood system such that every locally optimal tour is globally optimal. Then
--
--   $$\mathrm{P}=\mathrm{NP}.$$
--
--   Neighborhoods are generated by one polynomial-time procedure on the encoded instance and current tour. No bound is imposed on the number of local-search iterations.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: polynomial-size neighborhoods guarantee optimality → a uniform polynomial-time listing of every neighbor, and every local optimum globally optimal; reason: makes the implicit effective neighborhood convention explicit; cardinality alone would permit an optimum oracle
--   - encoding: general TSP with each vertex visited exactly once → permutations of Fin n with a symmetric nonnegative rational cost matrix and no triangle inequality; reason: the finite matrix encodes the undirected general TSP; a singleton has an idle tour, and diagonal entries are unused
-- source:
--   Report 7919/O, p. 7, §4 general TSP paragraph; survey [34]. Printed p. 7; PDF 9.

import Definitions.Def_VehicleRoutingSurvey_Routing_OptimizationModels

namespace VehicleRoutingSurvey.Routing

theorem N_neighborhood :
    (∃ neighbor, EffectiveNeighborhood generalTSP neighbor ∧
      ∀ x y, generalTSP.valid x → LocalOptimum generalTSP neighbor x y →
        ∀ z, generalTSP.feasible x z → generalTSP.cost x y ≤ generalTSP.cost x z) →
    ClassesEqual := by sorry

end VehicleRoutingSurvey.Routing
